{{Main|2002 United States Senate election in Alabama}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from Alabama}}
Since around 1980, Alabama voters had increasingly voted for Republican candidates at the federal level, especially in Presidential elections. By contrast, Democratic candidates had been elected to many state-level offices and comprised a longstanding majority in the [[Alabama Legislature]].

Incumbent Republican [[Jeff Sessions]] was not challenged in the primary,<ref name="Tanner">{{cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/news/nation/2002/06/05/primaries.htm|title=Primaries set stage for crucial Nov. vote|last=Tanner|first=Robert|date=June 5, 2002|work=[[USA Today]]|access-date=May 27, 2010|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121026002907/https://usatoday30.usatoday.com/news/nation/2002/06/05/primaries.htm|archive-date=October 26, 2012|agency=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref> and comfortably won re-election to a second term over [[Alabama State Auditor|state Auditor]] [[Susan Parker]] .<ref name="clerkresults">{{cite web|url=https://clerk.house.gov/member_info/electioninfo/2002election.pdf|title=Statistics of the Congressional Election of November 5, 2002|date=May 1, 2003|publisher=Office of the [[Clerk of the United States House of Representatives|Clerk of the House of Representatives]]|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070530194743/https://clerk.house.gov/member_info/electioninfo/2002election.pdf|archive-date=May 30, 2007|access-date=November 17, 2017}}</ref>

Sessions was not challenged in the primary.<ref name="Tanner"/>

