Incumbent Democrat [[Max Cleland]] ran for re-election to a second term, but lost to Republican U.S. Representative [[Saxby Chambliss]].

Chambliss's campaign used the refrain of [[defense (military)|national defense]] and security, but drew criticism for television ads that paired images of Cleland and [[Osama bin Laden]] and [[Saddam Hussein]], and for questioning the commitment to [[homeland security]] of his opponent, a triple amputee and decorated [[Vietnam War|Vietnam]] veteran.<ref>{{Cite AV media|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tKFYpd0q9nE|title=Chambliss Ad (Cleland)|date=August 2, 2006|publisher=Saxby Chambliss|orig-year=2002|via=YouTube}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2008/11/cleland-ad-causes-trouble-for-chambliss-015561|title=Cleland ad causes trouble for Chambliss|last=Barr|first=Andy|date=November 12, 2008|work=[[Politico]]|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160311215233/http://www.politico.com/story/2008/11/cleland-ad-causes-trouble-for-chambliss-015561|archive-date=March 11, 2016}}</ref> Republican Senator [[John McCain]] of [[Arizona]] said of one ad: "It's worse than disgraceful. It's reprehensible."<ref>''The Atlantic'': The Daily Dish: [https://www.theatlantic.com/daily-dish/archive/2008/11/quote-for-the-day/208733/ Quote for the Day.]  November 12, 2008.</ref> McCain, along with Republican Senator [[Chuck Hagel]] of Nebraska, made significant complaints to the Republican National Committee until the ads were taken down.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/news/os-xpm-2004-06-13-0406130249-story.html|title=Ex-Senator Boosts Kerry, Battles Critics|last=Lytle|first=Tamara|date=June 13, 2004|work=[[Orlando Sentinel]]|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191007063952/https://www.orlandosentinel.com/news/os-xpm-2004-06-13-0406130249-story.html|archive-date=October 7, 2019}}</ref> Nevertheless, Chambliss defeated Cleland by nearly seven percentage points.

