Incumbent Republican [[Susan Collins (politician)|Susan Collins]] comfortably won re-election to a second term.

[[Chellie Pingree]], State Senator and Senate Majority Leader attacked Collins for supporting the [[Bush tax cuts]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=9KVJAAAAIBAJ&pg=4390,4071363&dq=susan+collins+pingree&hl=en|title=Senate rivals clash on federal tax cuts|last=Griffin|first=Walter|date=October 30, 2002|work=[[Bangor Daily News]]|access-date=December 20, 2017|page=B1|via=Google News}}</ref> Both candidates opposed the [[Iraq War of 2003|Iraq War]] in the fall of 2002.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=gJk0AAAAIBAJ&pg=1507,4158086&dq=susan+collins&hl=en|title=Collins, Pingree oppose invasion|date=August 24, 2002|work=[[Sun Journal (Lewiston, Maine)|Sun Journal]]|access-date=December 20, 2017|page=B4|via=Google News}}</ref> However, Collins then supported the congressional resolution to attack Iraq, while Pingree opposed it.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=76VJAAAAIBAJ&pg=3854,1996336&dq=susan+collins+pingree&hl=en|title=Collins, Pingree divided on Iraq|last=Griffin|first=Walter|date=October 23, 2002|work=[[Bangor Daily News]]|access-date=December 20, 2017|page=B1|via=Google News}}</ref>

Collins, a popular moderate, was supported by some [[health care]] groups, [[environmentalists]] and [[Gay rights activist|gay rights advocates]]. She handily defeated State Senator [[Chellie Pingree]] of [[North Haven, Maine|North Haven]] in one of the few U.S. Senate elections in which both major parties nominated women in U.S. history.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://edition.cnn.com/2002/ALLPOLITICS/11/05/elec02.me.s.hotrace/|title=Collins wins re-election in Maine, CNN projects|date=November 6, 2002|access-date=December 20, 2017|publisher=[[CNN]]}}</ref>

