{{Main|2002 United States Senate election in Minnesota}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from Minnesota}}
Incumbent Democrat [[Paul Wellstone]] was running for re-election to a third term, but [[Death of Paul Wellstone|died]] in a plane crash eleven days before the election. The [[Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party]] (DFL) chose former [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]] and 1984 Presidential candidate [[Walter Mondale]] to replace Wellstone on the ballot. Mondale lost to Republican [[List of mayors of Saint Paul, Minnesota|Saint Paul Mayor]] [[Norm Coleman]]. The day before the election, [[Independence Party of Minnesota|Independence]] Governor [[Jesse Ventura]] had appointed [[Dean Barkley]] (IP) to serve the rest of Wellstone's term.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.foxnews.com/story/mondale-concedes-to-coleman | title=Mondale Concedes to Coleman | publisher=FOX News Network, LLC | agency=Associated Press | date=November 6, 2002 | access-date=August 12, 2010 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080515134959/http://www.foxnews.com/story/0,2933,69286,00.html | archive-date=May 15, 2008 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> {{As of|2023}}, this was the last Senate election in Minnesota won by a Republican.

In the primaries, Paul Wellstone defeated Dick Franson 93% to 5% and Norm Coleman defeated Jack Shepard 95% to 5%.

At the time of his death, Wellstone was slightly ahead in the polls. After Walter Mondale was chosen as the DFL candidate, in a poll taken a few days before the election Mondale was leading 51% to 45%. Early on [[Election Day (United States)|Election Day]], Mondale was leading in votes. By nightfall, however, Norm Coleman pulled ahead, winning by 49.5 percent to 47.3 percent.

Paul Wellstone still appeared on the ballot despite his death, despite a court order replacing Wellstone's name with Mondale's.

