Thune, who was considered a rising star in his party, ran against Tim Johnson, who narrowly won his first senate election in 1996. Thune launched a television advertising campaign mentioning [[al Qaeda]] and [[Saddam Hussein]], controversially contending that both were seeking nuclear weapons and that the country needed a missile defense system, something Johnson voted against 29 times and that Thune supported. Johnson attacked Thune for politicizing national security.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://0-infoweb.newsbank.com.wave.lccc.edu/iw-search/we/InfoWeb?p_product=NewsBank&p_theme=aggregated5&p_action=doc&p_docid=0F6804A403B019B7&p_docnum=13&p_queryname=22|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120707220212/http://0-infoweb.newsbank.com.wave.lccc.edu/iw-search/we/InfoWeb?p_product=NewsBank&p_theme=aggregated5&p_action=doc&p_docid=0F6804A403B019B7&p_docnum=13&p_queryname=22|url-status=dead|archive-date=July 7, 2012|title=Rothrock Library<!-- Bot generated title -->|access-date=December 20, 2017}}</ref> President [[George W. Bush]] campaigned for Thune in late October.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://0-infoweb.newsbank.com.wave.lccc.edu/iw-search/we/InfoWeb?p_product=NewsBank&p_theme=aggregated5&p_action=doc&p_docid=0F6BEB729417F6F0&p_docnum=19&p_queryname=22|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120707063934/http://0-infoweb.newsbank.com.wave.lccc.edu/iw-search/we/InfoWeb?p_product=NewsBank&p_theme=aggregated5&p_action=doc&p_docid=0F6BEB729417F6F0&p_docnum=19&p_queryname=22|url-status=dead|archive-date=July 7, 2012|title=Rothrock Library<!-- Bot generated title -->|access-date=December 20, 2017}}</ref> More than $20 million was spent in the election. Both candidates had raised over $5 million each.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://0-infoweb.newsbank.com.wave.lccc.edu/iw-search/we/InfoWeb?p_product=NewsBank&p_theme=aggregated5&p_action=doc&p_docid=12DD1BD23AD1CCE0&p_docnum=14&p_queryname=22|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120701102420/http://0-infoweb.newsbank.com.wave.lccc.edu/iw-search/we/InfoWeb?p_product=NewsBank&p_theme=aggregated5&p_action=doc&p_docid=12DD1BD23AD1CCE0&p_docnum=14&p_queryname=22|url-status=dead|archive-date=July 1, 2012|title=Rothrock Library<!-- Bot generated title -->|access-date=December 20, 2017}}</ref>

Johnson narrowly prevailed over Thune by a mere 524 votes. Despite the extreme closeness of the election, Thune did not contest the results and conceded defeat on the late afternoon of November 9. Johnson's narrow victory may be attributed to his strong support in [[Oglala Lakota County]]. Thune also underperformed in typically Republican areas. Johnson was sworn in for a second term on January 3, 2003. Thune would later be elected to the other Senate seat in [[2004 United States Senate election in South Dakota|2004]], defeating Minority Leader [[Tom Daschle]]. He would then serve with Johnson in 2005.

