{{Main|2002 United States Senate election in West Virginia}}

{{See also|List of United States senators from West Virginia}}
Incumbent Democrat [[Jay Rockefeller]] easily won re-election to a fourth term over Republican [[West Virginia State Senate|state Senator]] [[Jay Wolfe]].

Rockefeller was the heavy favorite, with one poll showed him leading 72% to 17%.<ref>{{cite news |id={{ProQuest|331821155}} |last1=Wrenn |first1=Deanna |title=Poll puts incumbent in the lead; Wolfe says he still plans on beating Rockefeller Nov. 5 |newspaper=Charleston Daily Mail |date=29 October 2002 |page=1A }}</ref> Rockefeller had $2.9 million cash on hand to Wolfe at $100,536 (In mid-October). Wolfe was endorsed by President [[George W. Bush]] and the [[National Rifle Association of America]] (NRA), but it wasn't enough to make the election competitive. Rockefeller won by a margin of over 25 percent.<ref>{{cite news |id={{ProQuest|331099095}} |last1=McCreary |first1=Joedy |title=Wolfe hopes grass-roots push unseats Rockefeller |newspaper=The Charleston Gazette |date=1 November 2002 |page=3A }}</ref>

